where $\sigma(c, a) \in \{0, 1\}$ is a source-trace indicator: $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace that produced answer $a$, and 0 otherwise. Discriminative signal arises from the MAX-SAT stage: when constraints from different traces conflict, Z3 excludes low-weight constraints from $\mathcal{C}^*$, and the scorer attributes these exclusions to the corresponding source answers via $\sigma$ (see Algorithm~2 in Appendix~\ref{app:z3-pseudocode}).

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Answer scoring.} Let $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$ denote the excluded constraint set. Each candidate answer $a \in \mathcal{A}(q)$ is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) \;-\; \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) \in \{0, 1\}$ is a source-trace indicator: $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace that produced answer $a$, and 0 otherwise. The penalty weight $\alpha = 0.5$ in all experiments. The first term rewards answers whose source constraints are satisfied; the second penalizes answers whose source constraints were excluded by MAX-SAT. Discriminative signal arises when constraints from different traces conflict: Z3 excludes low-weight constraints from $\mathcal{C}^*$, and the scorer attributes these exclusions to the corresponding source answers via $\sigma$ (see Algorithm~2 in Appendix~\ref{app:z3-pseudocode}).

tool_args.old_string:
In practice, self-extracted constraints are tightly coupled to their source answer: $c_m$ derived from trace $t_i$ typically entails $a_i$ ($\sigma(c_m, a_i) \approx 1$). When constraints lack discriminative power across candidates, the score function degenerates:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
which reduces to a weighted count of traces supporting $a$, equivalent to majority voting when weights are uniform.

